In recent years, molecule-based porous compounds, called metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) or porous coordination polymers (PCPs), [1] have attracted widespread attention for applications such as gas-handling and storage operations,[2] gas separation,[3] and gas sensors.[4] The emergence of electronically conductive MOFs (EC-MOFs), constructed by the periodic connection of metal ions and redox-active organic units,[5] enables them to overcome the long-standing issue as to whether conductive stable porous structures can be used toward electrical applications.[6] As for semiconductor devices, dozens of EC-MOFs with permanent porosity and conductivity have been developed.[7] Among them, two dimensional (2D) π- conjugated EC-MOFs showed exciting semiconductor properties as active materials with satisfactory stability.[4b, 6a] The coexistence of crystallinity, porosity, and conductivity creates a platform that can be used for a variety of applications, such as electrocatalysis,[8] supercapacitors,[9] chemiresistive gas sensors, [10] and field effect transistors (FETs).[11]
However, state of the art 2D π-conjugation EC-MOFs have been limited to single redox-active organic ligand systems,[6a, 7a] which limits the tunability of both conductivity and topology; thus, it becomes difficult to meet the various requirements for electronic applications. We designed the electronic and porous structure of an EC-MOF using two types of organic π-conjugated ligands. The use of two ligand types provided us the ability to create specific properties and functionalities. Typically, the 2D π- conjugated EC-MOFs belong to a subclass of hexagonal / tetragonal layered structures and are based on trigonal/square organic ligands and square planar mononuclear metal nodes.[7a, 12] Through selecting the appropriate combination of binary trigonal organic ligands, both “through-bond” pathways in 2D sheets and “through-space” transport between continuously conjugated stacking layers can be modulated, which changes the conductivity and porosity via electron delocalization efficiency and channel shapes, respectively.
In this work, we report that a combination of 2,3,6,7,10,11- hexahydrotriphenylene (HHTP, molecular size, ${\sim}0.97$ nm) ligands in hexagonal $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ [12b] and tetrahydroxy-1,4- quinone (THQ, molecular size, ${\sim}0.55\quad\mathsf{n m})$ ligands in orthorhombic ${\mathsf{C u}}_{3}({\mathsf{T H Q}})_{2}$ [7b] into one material can form a new dual-ligand based 2D π-conjugation EC-MOF, $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})(\mathsf{T H Q})$ (Figure 1a). The uniform hybridizing of the two trigonal organic ligands (containing Cu of two sizes) based EC-MOF was expected to form a unique trigonal crystal structure and ordered arrangement of redox-active ligands on the nanoscale. This was hypothesized to facilitate improved room temperature (RT) sensing properties in terms of both the electronic properties (low baseline) and surface chemistry (highly accessible surface area). As a proof of concept, the chemiresistive gas sensing properties of this construct will be studied the structure-properties relationships compared with pristine $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ at RT.
Compared with other reported single-ligand based $2\mathsf{D}\ \uppi.$ conjugation EC-MOFs, the square planar building unit, $\mathsf{C u O}_{4}$ of $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})(\mathsf{T H Q})$ is more complicated. This complexity results from the large difference between the two ligands: coordination capability and molecular size. In addition, many factors in the synthetic route to $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})(\mathsf{T H Q})$ , such as temperature, reaction time, the ratio of HHTP to THQ, and metal salts were found to dramatically affect the obtained product. All these factors increase the difficulty in controlling the purities, crystallinity, and desired structure of the final EC-MOF. Experimentally, HHTP shows much stronger coordination with $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ than THQ (Figure S1); and thus, crystalline $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ impurities can easily form during the reaction. Simply increasing the concentration of THQ merely increases the $C u_{3}(T H Q)_{2}$ impurities. Therefore, ethylenediamine (en), which can strongly chelate to $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ and form the soluble coordination complex $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{e n})_{2},\mathsf{I7b l}$ was chosen as the base to balance the coordination capability of HHTP and THQ with $\mathsf{C u}^{2+}$ via a competing bond formation mechanism during the preparation of the $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})(\mathsf{T H Q})$ dual-ligand semiconductor (Figure 1, see Supporting Information (SI) for details). The failure on obtaining $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})(\mathsf{T H Q})$ also indicate the possible template role of en due to the suitable length of the spacer in the molecule (Figure S2 and S3). After optimization of the reaction conditions, pure and crystalline black powders of $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{H H T P})(\mathsf{T H Q})$ nanowires were obtained (Figure S4 and S5, see SI for details).
Figure 2a shows the uniform morphological features of the $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})(\mathsf{T H Q})$ nanowires $(20-50~\mathsf{n m}$ diameter, hundreds of nm long) obtained at $24\ h$ . EDS mapping images clearly reveal the coexistence of Cu and O elements. Together with the simulated crystal structure, synchrotron powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD, $\lambda=0.79962\mathrm{~\AA~}$ ) patterns refined by the Pawley method indicated a trigonal unit cell $(\mathsf{P3}\mathsf{m}\mathsf{1})$ with cell parameters of $\mathsf{a}=$ $\mathsf{b}=17.154\mathrm{\AA}$ , and $\mathsf{c}=6.266\mathsf{A}$ (Figure 2b and 2c, Table S1, see SI for details). The AB slipped-parallel model with slightly slipped stacking of every two layers was energetically favored (Figure S6). The broad (002) peak at $2\Theta=14.68^{\circ}$ $28.48^{\circ}$ for $\$123$ Å) indicated short range ordering along the c-axis compared with the narrow and sharp (100) peak at $2\theta\ =3.08^{\circ}$ $\cdot5.95^{\circ}$ for $\lambda=$ 1.54178 Å) of the ab plane, which is commonly observed for 2D π-conjugation PCP/MOFs.[6b, 7b, 12b, 13] The ${\sf N}_{2}$ sorption isotherm at $77~\mathsf{K}$ of $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})(\mathsf{T H Q})$ shows a BET surface area of ${\sim}441.2~\mathsf{m}^{2}~\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ (Figure 2d), revealing an intrinsically high porosity that cannot be achieved simply through a nonporous AB stacking structure with $60^{\circ}$ rotations between neighboring layers or a significantly parallel slipped structure (Figure S7 and S8). High resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) and the selected area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern further supports the assertion that the nanowires are growing along caxis and show a typical lattice spacing of (100) facets (Figure 2e). A honeycomb structure with a pore size of approximately $1.3\mathrm{-}1.4~\mathsf{n m}$ was observed by HRTEM from the c-axis (Figure 2f) and the in-plane periodicity was also evident from the corresponding fast Fourier transform (FFT) analysis (Figure 2f, inset). In contrast to the uniformly distributed ligands of the same size in single-ligand based $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ ,[12b, 14] three symmetrical, but isolated, black areas, corresponding to large HHTP ligands (marked by three triangle shapes in Figure 2f) in a honeycomb uniformly surround the pore can be observed in high resolution areas. This is consistent with the simulated trigonal structure containing uniformly dispersed large HHTP ligands and small THQ ligands. These results also rule out the formation of $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2/3}(\mathsf{T H Q})_{4/3}$ and $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{4/3}(\mathsf{T H Q})_{2/3}$ , which should show different pore structures and lattice spacings (Figure S9).
Elemental analysis (EA) and thermogravimetric analysis (TG/DTA) were used to verify the charge and composition of $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})(\mathsf{T H Q})$ . The as-prepared sample had a high percentage of N, up to ${\sim}4.6~\mathrm{wt.}\%$ (Table S2 and S3). We attributed the presence of N to the protonated en $(N H_{3}C H_{2}C H_{2}N H_{3}^{2+})$ used in the reaction. Similar results were reported, the presence of [Cu3 $(\mathsf{C}_{6}\mathsf{O}_{6})_{2}$ $(N H_{3}C H_{2}C H_{2}N H_{3})_{1.36}]$ , by Bao’s group during their work that employed en as a copper chelating agent.[7b] En could not be completely removed, even by hot water treatment or Soxhlet extraction by methanol, possibly because it served as a counter ion to neutralize the negatively charged $\mathsf{C u O}_{4}$ (Figure S10 and S11).[7b, 12b, 15] The corresponding EA results suggested a chemical formula of $[\mathsf{C u}_{3}$ $(C_{18}0_{6}H_{6})$ $(\mathsf{C}_{6}\mathsf{O}_{6})$ $({\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}})_{11.78}$ $N H_{3}C H_{2}C H_{2}N H_{3})_{1.59}]$ for $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})(\mathsf{T H Q})$ (Table S2). In combination with calculations using the positively charged protonated en $(1.59\times2/3)$ , each square planar $\mathsf{C u O}_{4}$ center in the $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})(\mathsf{T H Q})$ framework was negatively charged $(-1)$ . To further confirm the composition and study the thermal stability of $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})(\mathsf{T H Q})$ , we examined the assynthesized product by TG-DTA. The mass loss value of guest molecules $(\sim20.4\%$ ; calcd. $21.5\%$ for loss of ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}_{\ \mathrm{\Gamma}}^{\prime}$ before ${\sim}200~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ and total mass loss of ${\sim}42.3\%$ at $500^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ (calcd. $47.4\%$ mass loss for final products of Cu-carbon mixtures) was consistent with the EA-deduced formula shown in Table S2 (Figure S12). Good thermal stability, up to ${\sim}300~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ in ${\sf N}_{2}$ and ${\sim}180\quad^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ in air, was also observed (Figure S13). The temperature dependent PXRD patterns in air further confirmed the good thermal stability of $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})(\mathsf{T H Q})$ crystals (Figure 3a). This indicates the strong potential for application of such materials in various electronic devices that must operate over a broad temperature range.
To investigate the electronic properties of $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})(\mathsf{T H Q}),$ conductivity measurements were performed (Figure 3b and 3c). As shown in Figure 3b, the linear and zero drift of the temperature dependent $1-V$ curves indicate good ohmic contact between the $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})(\mathsf{T H Q})$ pellets and the Pt electrodes (2 probe method). To avoid the influence of humidity and other interfering gases, this experiment was performed in an Ar-filled glove box. The increase in conductivity $(\sigma)$ with increasing temperature revealed the semiconducting behavior of the $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})(\mathsf{T H Q})$ powder pellet. A semi-conductivity value of $\sim2.53\times10^{-5}\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}$ was observed, which was a slightly higher than that in air with $35\%$ RH $(\sim5\times10^{-6}\:\mathsf{S}\:\mathsf{c m}^{-1})$ , and between that of ${\mathsf{C u}}_{3}({\mathsf{T H Q}})_{2}$ $(\sim10^{-8}\ \mathsf{S}\ \mathsf{c m}^{-1})$ and $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ $(\sim10^{-3}\ \mathsf{s}$ $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}.$ ).[7b, 10a] Similarly, a medium thermal activation energy of $0.30~\mathsf{e V}$ could be estimated from the linear fitting of logσ vs $\boldsymbol{\mathscr{1}/\intercal}$ (Figure 3c): 0.16 eV for $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ and 0.46 eV for $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{T H Q})_{2}$ .[7b, 10a] Figure 3d shows the current vs. time plots of the $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})(\mathsf{T H Q})$ powder pellet obtained by the DC reverse polarity method $(-\mathsf{x V}$ applied first on the pellet and then the polarity is quickly reversed to $+\mathsf{x V}$ $\mathsf{x V},\mathsf{x}=1,2,3,4$ and 5).[16] For all curves ranging from $\boldsymbol{\mathscr{I}}\cup$ to 5V, the lagging ionic current peak was not observed. Thus, ions are not the dominant charge carrier and the $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})(\mathsf{T H Q})$ is confirmed to be the electronically conductive material.
The semi-conductivity and low activation energy indicated that $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})(\mathsf{T H Q})$ might possess RT gas sensing properties. Accordingly, $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})(\mathsf{T H Q})$ nanowires were used as the active material of a RT chemiresistive gas sensor (Figure 4). The sensing measurements were conducted using a homemade dynamic sensing setup (see SI for details).[10a, 17] The $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})(\mathsf{T H Q})$ nanowire–thick film gas sensors were fabricated on insulating substrates with pre-deposited Au interdigital electrodes by the drop-coating method followed by activation under flowing dry air at RT. Figure 4a, S14, and S15 show the dynamic sensing curves of the $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})(\mathsf{T H Q})$ sensor over a broad range of $N H_{3}$ concentrations (1 ppm to saturated vapor) with good response-recovery properties at RT (response and recovery time are 1.65 min and 2.57 min toward 10 ppm $N H_{3}$ , respectively). Notably, the sensor showed good discrimination of $N H_{3}$ in the low concentration range (1–100 ppm). This capability has yet to be demonstrated by $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H H T P})_{2}$ ${\mathsf{C u}}_{3}(\mathsf{H I T P})_{2}$ , and $N i_{3}(H\mathsf{I T P})_{2}$ powder-based sensors to date.[10, 13] This might be due to the successful decrease in conductivity baseline of the active sensing materials (by about two orders of magnitude compared with single-ligand based EC-MOFs), enabling more sensitivity to small charge transfers caused by low concentration gas adsorption.
